  "body": "## What was wrong\n\nAgent harnesses verify their endpoint before every run with one tiny non-streaming request and read `response[\"model\"]` back, to catch a proxy that quietly serves a different model than the one asked for:\n\n```json\n{\"model\": \"sonnet\", \"max_tokens\": 16, \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Say OK\"}]}\n```\n\nThe live request log holds **1,563 of them over four days**. Every one was a real upstream call — some over two minutes — spent to learn a fact MCC knows before it opens a socket.\n\n## The mechanism\n\nTwo parts.\n\n**Detecting the probe.** `is_model_routing_probe_request` is deliberately narrow in every dimension a real conversation differs in: no tools, no system text, not streaming, `max_tokens` present and `<= 32`, exactly one `user` turn whose text is `say ok` or `say ok.` after casefolding and stripping. A user who types \"Say OK\" into a chat carries a real output budget and usually history, so they are never caught.\n\n**Naming the right model.** This is the part that is easy to get subtly wrong. The optimization intercept fires on `plan.primary`, but the executor tries the first attempt the route health registry has *not* benched (`application/execution.py`, `usable_indexes(...)`). So with the primary ejected, real traffic goes to a fallback while a naive local reply still names the primary — reporting the exact substitution the probe exists to detect as a clean bill of health.\n\n`ProviderExecutor.first_usable_attempt(plan)` now exposes the executor's own ordering (same `usable_indexes`, same `provider_lookup`), and the probe's reply carries that attempt's `provider_model`. Other optimization rules are untouched.\n\n### Which identity the normal reply echoes (verified, not assumed)\n\nThe probe must compare like with like, so this was grepped rather than guessed:\n\n- `application/routing.py:598-599` — `routed = request.model_copy(deep=True); routed.model = resolved.provider_model`\n- `application/execution.py` hands that same `routed.request` to the provider\n- `providers/openai_chat/provider.py:1165-1171` — `AnthropicStreamLedger(self._message_id, self._request.model, ...)`\n- `core/anthropic/streaming/ledger.py:172` (`self.model = model`) and `:196` (`\"model\": self.model` inside `message_start`)\n\nSo a normal streamed reply echoes the **bare upstream model id** (`minimax/minimax-m3-free`), never the `provider/model` ref (`commandcode/minimax/minimax-m3-free`). The probe reply now carries the same kind of identity.\n\n## Verification\n\nAn isolated server on `127.0.0.1:8199` with a scratch `HOME` holding a copy of the real `.env`. The live 8082 server, `~/.fcc` and the primary checkout were not touched. The **actual** `verify_model_routing` from `autonomous-agent-sdk-template/client.py` was executed against it.\n\n**Rule on:**\n\n```\nelapsed_seconds=0.656\n{'requested': 'sonnet', 'served': 'minimax/minimax-m3-free', 'mismatch': True}\n```\n\nIts row in the isolated `requests.db`:\n\n```\n{'id': 'req_d65cd026...', 'provider': None, 'optimization': 'probe_auto_response'}\nrequest_attempts rows: 0\n```\n\n**Rule off (`ENABLE_PROBE_AUTO_RESPONSE=false` + restart), same call:**\n\n```\nelapsed_seconds=11.212\n{'id': 'req_af570b0d...', 'provider': 'commandcode', 'optimization': None}\nrequest_attempts rows: 6\n  attempt 0 commandcode/minimax/minimax-m3-free  succeeded  10611.6 ms\n  attempt 1 nous_portal/meituan/longcat-2.0:free skipped    never reached\n  attempt 2 nous_portal/tencent/hy3:free         skipped    never reached\n  attempt 3 opencode/hy3-free                    skipped    never reached\n  attempt 4 open_router/z-ai/glm-5.2:free        skipped    never reached\n  attempt 5 nvidia_nim/nvidia/nemotron-3-...     skipped    never reached\n```\n\n0.66 s and no upstream row, against 11.2 s and a real chain.\n\n**UI opened, not assumed.** `http://127.0.0.1:8199/admin` -> Token Optimizer renders the new row under **Local rules**: \"Model routing probe\", state `on`, fired `1`, tokens avoided `6`, answers with `\"OK\"`; the cache table shows it as `answered locally · probe auto response`.\n\n**Suite:** `15 failed, 7622 passed, 38 skipped`. `ruff format`, `ruff check` and `ty check` clean repo-wide (`scripts/ci.sh`).\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- A client that sends exactly `Say OK` with `max_tokens <= 32`, no system text, no tools and no history gets a local `OK`. That shape is a probe by construction, but if one ever is not, `ENABLE_PROBE_AUTO_RESPONSE=false` restores the previous behaviour exactly.\n- The probe no longer proves upstream *liveness* — only routing. The run's first real request proves liveness anyway, and fails visibly where a synthetic `OK` would not. This is stated in the code and in the docs.\n- `first_usable_attempt` consults `provider_lookup` in `rate_based` mode, which resolves a provider object to read its throttle. No network call, no request; the integration test asserts `preflight_stream` and `stream_response` were never called rather than that the resolver was never touched.\n- The reply names the model the executor *would* try first. If the route's health changes between the probe and the run's first request, the two can disagree — the same window the real request already lives with.\n\n## Note for reviewers\n\nThe local suite has **15 pre-existing failures** that also fail on unmodified `main`: 8 websearch tests reading the real `~/.fcc/.env`, 6 PowerShell installer tests, and `tests/runtime/test_provider_manager.py::test_catalog_publication_tracks_warm_refresh_and_direct_cache`. They pass on GitHub runners, which have no `~/.fcc`.\n\n## Deliberately not done\n\nNo GitHub release — this version is batched with another PR by the orchestrator.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01MXPGmiD5B4KEyCXT6WYsrm\n",
