    "message": "feat: answer client model-routing probes locally, echoing the model that would answer\n\nAgent harnesses check their endpoint before every run with one tiny\nnon-streaming request -- `{\"model\": <requested>, \"max_tokens\": 16, \"messages\":\n[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Say OK\"}]}` -- and read `response[\"model\"]` to\ncatch a proxy that quietly serves a different model than the one asked for.\nThe live request log holds 1,563 of them over four days. Every one was a real\nupstream call, some taking over two minutes, to learn a fact MCC already knows\nbefore it opens a socket.\n\nMCC now answers that probe itself. `is_model_routing_probe_request` is narrow\nin every dimension a real conversation differs in: no tools, no system text,\nnot streaming, `max_tokens` present and <= 32, exactly one user turn whose text\nis \"say ok\" or \"say ok.\" after casefolding. A user who types \"Say OK\" into a\nchat carries a real output budget and usually history, so they are never\ncaught. `ENABLE_PROBE_AUTO_RESPONSE=false` restores the old behaviour exactly.\n\nThe non-obvious half is which model the reply names. The optimization intercept\nfires on `plan.primary`, but the executor picks the first attempt the route\nhealth registry has not benched -- so with the primary ejected, real traffic\ngoes to a fallback while a naive reply would still name the primary. That is\nprecisely the substitution the probe exists to detect, reported as a clean bill\nof health. `ProviderExecutor.first_usable_attempt` now exposes the executor's\nown `usable_indexes` ordering (same `provider_lookup`), and the probe's reply\ncarries that attempt's `provider_model`. That is also the identity a normal\nstreamed reply echoes in `message_start`: routing rewrites `request.model` to\n`resolved.provider_model` (application/routing.py:599) and the provider hands\nthat same string to the stream ledger (providers/openai_chat/provider.py:1167\n-> core/anthropic/streaming/ledger.py:172,196), so the harness compares like\nwith like -- a bare upstream model id, not a `provider/model` ref.\n\nVerification\n------------\nIsolated server on 127.0.0.1:8199 with a scratch HOME holding a copy of the\nreal .env; the live 8082 server and ~/.fcc were not touched. The actual\n`verify_model_routing` from autonomous-agent-sdk-template/client.py was\nexecuted against it:\n\n    elapsed_seconds=0.656\n    {'requested': 'sonnet', 'served': 'minimax/minimax-m3-free', 'mismatch': True}\n\nIts request log row: optimization='probe_auto_response', provider=None, and\nzero rows in request_attempts -- nothing reached a provider.\n\nWith ENABLE_PROBE_AUTO_RESPONSE=false and a restart, the same call:\n\n    elapsed_seconds=11.212\n\nand the log gains a real chain: attempt 0 commandcode/minimax/minimax-m3-free\nsucceeded in 10,611 ms, five fallbacks \"never reached\".\n\nThe Token Optimizer page was opened at /admin and renders the new row: \"Model\nrouting probe\", on, fired 1, 6 tokens avoided, answers with \"OK\"; the request\ntable labels the row \"answered locally - probe auto response\".\n\nSuite: 15 failed, 7622 passed, 38 skipped -- the standing 15 pre-existing\nfailures (8 websearch reading the real ~/.fcc/.env, 6 PowerShell installer,\ntest_catalog_publication_tracks_warm_refresh_and_direct_cache), none new.\nruff format, ruff check and ty are clean repo-wide.\n\nNot done: no GitHub release; the orchestrator batches this version with\nanother PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MXPGmiD5B4KEyCXT6WYsrm",
